    "score": 139,
    "text": "Back in 2015, when I was still at Google, there was a small group of code-golf enthusiasts spending hours trying to save just one byte on their solutions for weekly code-golf problems. No matter how hard you tried, there always seemed to be another Googler who found a shorter solution. And once a problem entered the challenge phase and every solution became public, it was always astonishing to read how brilliant other people&#x27;s solutions were.<p>11 years later, LLMs are evolving fast. A recent OpenAI post (&quot;Perplexity and Intelligence&quot;, <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;openai.com&#x2F;index&#x2F;perplexity-and-intelligence&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;openai.com&#x2F;index&#x2F;perplexity-and-intelligence&#x2F;</a>) described researchers watching a model solve open math problems one after another in a Slack thread. I started to wonder: how would frontier models compete with the records that a small group of brilliant minds created with hours of effort each week?<p>So I built this benchmark. Frontier models do find a lot of new approaches (and very brilliant ones) to solve these problems. For example, in two problems (&quot;Surviving Queens&quot; and &quot;The Rolling Dice&quot;), frontier models use complex numbers to solve them in Python and Ruby (both languages have built-in complex number support). And for the &quot;(-5)-Smooth Numbers&quot; problem, where you have to write code to list the first n positive integers of the form (-2)^x\u22c5(-3)^y\u22c5(-5)^z, most solutions build them with nested loops or by growing a set, then sort. GPT-6.2 Astra gets them all from powers of 4, and earns a sole best with this brilliant solution:<p><pre><code>    g=lambda n:sorted(4**i%(2**999-5)%(2**49-3)for i in range(7000))[:n]\n</code></pre>\nThere is a dedicated section for this: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;bjin.github.io&#x2F;go-codegolf-benchmark&#x2F;#why&#x2F;finding-a-different-approach\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;bjin.github.io&#x2F;go-codegolf-benchmark&#x2F;#why&#x2F;finding-a-...</a><p>It&#x27;s nice to see GPT-6.2 Astra become the first frontier model to beat the human records in code golfing. Along with other frontier models, it finds a lot of interesting solutions that Googlers back in 2015 didn&#x27;t. On the other hand, I also believe code golfing can be a good benchmark (not a comprehensive one, of course) for frontier models.<p>Code golfing rewards thinking differently, because a shorter solution usually comes from looking at the problem from a different angle. It also rewards running experiments, not just thinking: writing a search program to find a shorter expression, or exploring lesser-known features and functions in the target compiler, interpreter, or library. Human code golfers do this a lot, and no public training data tells a frontier model how to do so.",
    "time": 1795365888,
    "title": "GPT-6.2 Astra is the first model to beat Googlers' code golf records",
    "type": "story",
    "url": "https://bjin.github.io/go-codegolf-benchmark/#leaderboard"
  },
